<div class="sec" id="s2">
  <div class="num">02</div>
  <h2>Before you let it change files</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    These are my four rules. Keep them even when the task looks boring.
  </p>
  <h3>Never paste secrets into a prompt</h3>
  <p>
    No passwords, bank details or other people's private information. No API
    keys either: these are secret codes that let software use an account or paid
    service. Put them only where the service's instructions say, never in a
    conversation or project file.
  </p>
  <h3>Read the plan before you approve</h3>
  <p>
    If it includes deleting files, installing software or going outside the
    project, ask why. You can say no. An instruction to wait is useful, but it
    is not a technical lock on what an agent can do.
  </p>
  <h3>Keep work in one project folder</h3>
  <p>
    Use a folder for this website, never your whole Documents or home folder.
    Keep private material out of it. Agents may send relevant file contents to
    the company providing the AI. A project folder helps limit the job; it does
    not guarantee the agent cannot reach elsewhere.
  </p>
  <h3>Make mistakes undoable</h3>
  <p>
    Use copies of photos, not your originals. Before changing a page you like,
    copy the whole project folder somewhere outside the working folder. Keep
    that backup closed to the agent. A separate working copy is useful, but it
    is not protection for everything on your computer.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Claude Code, leave permission prompts on. Do not choose automatic
    approval or bypass permissions. Delta's optional exercise explains its lack
    of those controls. If something feels off, stop approving actions and
    message me. Nothing in this folder is urgent enough to guess.
  </p>
</div>
